JavaScriptCore: JIT on 32-bit ARM with the softfp ABI (V8's model)

webOS TV userland is ARMv7 "softfp": a VFP unit is present, but the C calling convention passes
floating-point values in core registers. JSC's ARMv7 JIT requires hardfp (PlatformEnable.h), so
softfp builds fall back to the CLoop interpreter. JIT code itself keeps doubles in VFP registers;
only the boundary with C differs. V8 handles this at its C-call boundary (MovToFloatParameter,
MovFromFloatResult, CalculateStackPassedWords), and JSC used to for 32-bit iOS (see the Darwin
ARM_THUMB2 UnaryDoubleOpWrapper in ThunkGenerators.cpp). Do the same here:

- WTF: CPU(ARM_SOFTFP_VFP) = VFP present, softfp calling convention. Allow ENABLE_JIT with it.
- CCallHelpers (Baseline/DFG operation calls): a double argument goes in the next even core
  register pair (r0:r1, r2:r3) or on the stack 8-byte aligned, never back-filling r3 (AAPCS C.3);
  a double result is read from r0:r1.
- WasmToJS: operationConvertToF32/F64 results come back in r0 / r0:r1.
- WebAssembly BBQ is off by default on softfp (its C calls still assume hardfp); OMG is already
  off on ARMv7. WebAssembly runs on IPInt, whose C calls pass no floating-point values.

LLInt and IPInt pass only pointers/ints to C; the JIT-to-JIT conventions (JSToWasm/WasmToJS
argument shuffles) do not involve the C ABI and are unchanged.

